      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "The aim of the present study was to investigate the predictive value of preprocedural monocyte count-to-high-density lipoprotein cholesterol ratio (MHR) on development of in-stent restenosis in patients undergoing coronary bare-metal stent (BMS) implantation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS & METHODS",
          "content": "Data from 705 patients who had undergone BMS implantation and additional control coronary angiography were analyzed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Patients were divided into three tertiles based on preprocedural MHR. Restenosis occurred in 59 patients (25%) in the lowest tertile, 84 (35%) in the middle tertile and 117 (50%) in the highest MHR tertile (p < 0.001). Using multiple logistic regression analysis, smoking, diabetes mellitus, stent length, preprocedural MHR and C-reactive protein levels emerged as independent predictors of in-stent restenosis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "High preprocedural MHR is related to BMS restenosis."
        }
      ]},
